{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary (bug)\nThe host-local hourly updater (`~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh`, NOT in this repo) pruned\nnon-current release dirs with an unconditional `rm -rf`, deleting code out from under running\nrebar processes (lazy imports then fail mid-op). Seen twice in the wild; the updater was PAUSED\nas a stopgap, leaving this host drifting from `origin/main`.\n\n## The hard constraint\nTwo goals conflict: (a) never delete a release a live process is using, AND (b) keep disk\nbounded (the `masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` disk-pressure incident). A liveness-only prune cannot\nreclaim under this host's continuous load; a retention-only prune can still delete a live\nrelease. The fix satisfies both. The full three-layer design, edge cases, execution steps,\nverification oracles, and rollback are recorded in the pinned implementation comment on this\nticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] [non-codebase] **The updater is RE-ENABLED on this host and confirmed running** — `launchctl list` shows\n      `com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update` again, and a forced run\n      (`launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update`) completes successfully.\n      This ticket is not done while the host is still drifting from `origin/main`.\n      provenance: environment=host-local macOS (this dev box, ~/.local/state/rebar-dev); principal=joeoakhart (operator); privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — launchctl bootstrap+list show com.navapbc.rebar-dev-update loaded; kickstart -k completed exit 0; a by-hand run built origin/main 6e282021, swapped current, installed shims.\n- [x] [non-codebase] A long-running `rebar` operation spanning at least one updater cycle COMPLETES successfully\n      while the updater is active — demonstrated, not argued. The natural probe is a real close\n      (whose LLM completion verifier takes minutes) with a forced update fired mid-run.\n      provenance: environment=host-local macOS; principal=joeoakhart; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — a live global rebar-mcp (via the shim) pinned its release across 3 forced kickstart cycles and stayed up; 12 continuous global-shim `rebar list` calls across 3 cycles → 0 failures; pinned release intact.\n- [x] [non-codebase] Disk usage remains BOUNDED under continuous rebar use: releases do not accumulate without\n      limit when operations are always in flight. State the bound (count or bytes) and show it\n      holding, so this does not recreate the `masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` disk-pressure incident.\n      provenance: environment=host-local sandbox exercising the real prune_releases() + real flock; principal=joeoakhart; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=simulation — 12 seeded dead releases, KEEP=3 → reclaimed to 4 (current+newest3), no alert; bound CAP=10×~346MB≈3.5GB; live run logged prune summary remaining=2.\n- [x] [non-codebase] When the two goals genuinely conflict — a release cannot be reclaimed because it is in use and\n      the retention bound is reached — the behaviour is an explicit ALERT, not a silent deletion and\n      not a silent unbounded grow. Name where that alert surfaces.\n      provenance: environment=host-local sandbox (real prune + flock + REBAR_DEV_ALERT_CMD capture) plus live SNS; principal=joeoakhart; privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=simulation — 13 flock-held releases >CAP=10 → release-cap alert emitted AND all live releases survived; unlocked control pruned to 2, no alert; live SNS publish to rebar-gerrit-alerts verified (MessageId bdc90c6e).\n- [x] [non-codebase] The chosen mechanism and its rationale are documented for the next operator,\n      including how to pause and resume, so nobody rediscovers this. The record lives in a host-local\n      operator doc that is deliberately git-ignored (via `.git/info/exclude`, pattern `*.local.md`)\n      and is NOT part of the committed repository tree.\n      provenance: environment=host-local macOS (`/Users/joeoakhart/rebar` working tree, untracked file); principal=joeoakhart (operator); privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — `git check-ignore -v` confirms the operator doc is excluded and on-disk (11699 bytes); it documents mechanism, tunables (RELEASES_KEEP/RELEASES_CAP), pause/resume via launchctl, alerting, rollback, and the failure signature. Not committable by design (it embeds host paths).\n- [x] [non-codebase] The failure signature is recorded where a future reader will find it: an\n      `ImportError` naming a module that exists in the source tree, at a `~/.local/state/rebar-dev/releases/`\n      path that no longer exists, means a pruned-mid-run release — not a packaging defect (two sessions\n      mis-diagnosed it as packaging). Recorded in the same host-local git-ignored operator doc, outside\n      the committed repository tree.\n      provenance: environment=host-local macOS (untracked operator doc under the working tree); principal=joeoakhart (operator); privilege_posture=production-equivalent; instrument=live-call — the operator doc's \"failure signature\" section spells out the ImportError-with-missing-release-path pattern and its correct diagnosis. Deliberately non-codebase (git-ignored host-local doc).\n\n## Scope note\nThe defective script `~/.local/bin/rebar-dev-update.sh` is HOST-LOCAL and outside this repository, so\nthe fix may land with no repo file impact. If investigation shows rebar itself should participate\n(for example a launcher that pins a resolved release for the process lifetime), that is a distinct\nin-repo change and should be filed separately rather than absorbed here.\n\nRelated: `shady-blueblack-whale` traces the same mechanism under the `uv tool install --force`\ntrigger; `frangible-irongray-adouri` (closed duplicate) carries the original traced RCA;\n`masonic-abeyant-stagbeetle` is the disk-pressure incident that constrains the retention side.\n\n\n---"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787241410544581001,"uuid":"e061e1d5-57a7-4553-b3dc-2db5a3022cc2"}